592186045417] [17592186105957 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105957 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105957 #edb/ref 1045 "The Angle Between Two Vectors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105957 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186106613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105957 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105957 #edb/ref 1050 1080.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105957 #edb/ref 1050 1080.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186105957 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b632e7a8-8c52-5b89-bc1d-2f451e2f7e82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106109 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106109 #edb/ref 1069 "q-19115" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106109 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106109 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the points $A(t,2,1)$, $B(2t,3,1)$ and $C(2,{t}^{2},-1).$ Find $∠BAC$ given that $\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}=2.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106109 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4e1c8f13-f8d5-58eb-9a61-227e3239a680" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106109 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106109 #edb/ref 1074 "Note that the position vectors of the points $A$, $B$, $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨t,\\,2,\\,1⟩,\\,b=⟨2t,\\,3,\\,1⟩,\\,c=⟨2,\\,{t}^{2},\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠BAC=\\theta .$\n\n![](images/28/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,3,\\,1⟩-⟨t,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,{t}^{2},\\,-1⟩-⟨t,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2-t,\\,{t}^{2}-2,\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}=2$, we can solve for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =2 \\\\ t\\cdot (2-t)+1\\cdot ({t}^{2}-2)+0\\cdot (-2) & =2 \\\\ 2t-{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}-2+0 & =2 \\\\ 2t-2 & =2 \\\\ 2t & =4 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨2,1,0⟩, \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =⟨2-2,\\,{2}^{2}-2,\\,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,2,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{8}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}})≈{71.57}^{∘}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106613 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106613 #edb/ref 1061 15820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106613 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "abecb670-0ed2-5399-a3bc-76a8818c1a52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106613 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106614 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106614 #edb/ref 1061 15822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106614 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e4bbf74b-4a08-54ea-990b-e835d453fe01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106614 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106615 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106615 #edb/ref 1061 15823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106615 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d6da2e77-5ad7-5fdb-8a14-14cfb45476ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106615 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106649 #edb/ref 1061 18783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106649 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "54ffd497-a19e-584a-b03d-5bad2ad173bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106649 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107072 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the two vectors $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 1,-2, 2 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 3,0,-1 \\rangle$ shown in the picture below.\n\n![](images/87/87395f5fe6657cec5dd98eed9c3c535a5f6dc9f259d4558c86a4d618c904e5c1.png)\n\nTo find the angle $\\theta$ between the vectors, we can rearrange the dot product formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =|\\,a\\,|\\cdot |\\,b\\,|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\ \\theta  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe dot product is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the magnitudes of $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\,| & =\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =3, \\\\ |\\,b\\,| & =\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos \\Bigg( \\dfrac{1}{3\\sqrt{10}} \\Bigg) \\approx 83.9^\\circ.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107072 #edb/ref 1065 2936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107072 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "14af2112-1d41-5da4-8262-4c1541fee57b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107072 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235819 #edb/ref 1080 "{90}^{∘}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235819 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235819 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "038e0307-c334-55eb-bdf7-692cdb33f023" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186