{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 3+(-3)\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}\\cdot \\sqrt{26}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{754}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{754}})≈{85.82}^{∘}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287925 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287925 #edb/ref 1069 "q-19107" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287925 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287925 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $∠BAC$ given the points $A(2,1,-1)$, $B(3,-2,2)$, and $C(-1,-2,1).$ Round your answer to the nearest degree." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287925 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ea49a8dd-e4b2-55bc-b471-da1f1d77c574" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287925 #edb/ref 1074 "Note that the position vectors of the points $A$, $B$, $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨2,1,-1⟩,\\,b=⟨3,-2,2⟩,\\,c=⟨-1,-2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠BAC=\\theta .$\n\n![](images/28/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,2⟩-⟨2,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-3,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,1⟩-⟨2,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,-3,2⟩\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (-3)+(-3)\\cdot (-3)+3\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{19}\\cdot \\sqrt{22}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{418}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{418}})≈{54}^{∘}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287926 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287926 #edb/ref 1069 "q-19119" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287926 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287926 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the points $D(1,3,2)$, $E(t,2,4)$ and $F(3,t,3).$ Find $∠EDF$ given that $\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{DF}=2.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287926 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "567d9479-bc13-5331-b757-cfe1cd8387a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287926 #edb/ref 1074 "Note that the position vectors of the points $D$, $E$, $F$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d=⟨1,\\,3,\\,2⟩,\\,e=⟨t,\\,2,\\,4⟩,\\,f=⟨3,\\,t,\\,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△DEF$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠EDF=\\theta .$\n\n![](images/c9/c9cfb3e84e6f76d243125f2a09246b2a1300a0134e71359bb2b528082f064f93.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =e-d \\\\  & =⟨t,\\,2,\\,4⟩-⟨1,\\,3,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t-1,\\,-1,\\,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =f-d \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,t,\\,3⟩-⟨1,\\,3,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,t-3,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{DF}=2$, we can solve for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =2 \\\\ (t-1)\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot (t-3)+2\\cdot 1 & =2 \\\\ 2t-2-t+3+2 & =2 \\\\ t & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =⟨-1-1,-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,-1,2⟩, \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =⟨2,-1-3,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-4,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{DF}}{|\\overset{\\to }{DE}||\\overset{\\to }{DF}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{9}\\cdot \\sqrt{21}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{21}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{21}})≈{81.64}^{∘}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333452 #edb/ref 1080 "{120}^{∘}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333452 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333452 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "321940d9-bd6b-529f-b36b-52de6034ea1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333509 #edb/ref 1080 "{90}^{∘}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333509 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333509 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "382cb607-2c08-5d7e-a967-c6d75cc084f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333578 #edb/ref 1080 "{45}^{∘}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333578 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333578 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3e71dcdb-a48d-57d3-a1bd-cdac86069077" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333653 #edb/ref 1080 "{30}^{∘}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333653 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333653 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "47c034fd-ff2c-563e-807c-0bd209a7f45d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333805 #edb/ref 1080 "{180}^{∘}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333805 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333805 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5927a3fa-7ca4-5835-8996-fbb98d090ec9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333842 #edb/ref 1080 "{60}^{∘}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333842 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333842 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5d29e042-7c4a-5adf-9528-5bd45b78ba7b" #ed